
// Coating Materials (material/coat/coating)
//
// The finishes laid over a part: anodize and the conversion coatings black
// oxide and phosphate, which grow out of the metal and keep its machining
// beneath; the interference films, steel's temper colours and anodized
// titanium, that colour a metal without hiding it; powder coat, from glossy
// with its orange peel to the textured wrinkle, sand and hammertone; paint
// over primer, chipped where the part wears; and hot-dip galvanizing's
// spangle of zinc crystals. Each returns material, color, height and slope,
// as the machined and animated materials do; the height is 0, and relief
// rides in the material as displacement.
//
// A coating is a layer, but a material here has one lobe: anodize is the metal
// seen through its dyed oxide, a tinted metal whose own faint glint, about 6%
// off the oxide, is left out; the paints and powders are opaque dielectrics.
//
// Doc convention follows builtin/base.gsl: `///` lines are public docs.

import "material";
import "texture";
import "noise";
import "color";
import "material/bulk/metal";

// --- Anodize and conversion coatings ---

/// anodized(baseMaterial, baseColor, dyeColor): aluminium anodized (Type II,
/// 5 to 25 um) and dyed `dyeColor`, white for clear. The oxide is clear and
/// the dye in its pores tints the light through it and back, so the part
/// stays a metal, its colour the metal's times the dye's, a little dimmed and
/// hazed by the oxide (judged). The oxide follows the surface, so the
/// machining beneath shows through: its lay and grooves are kept. Returns
/// material, color, height and slope.
anodized(baseMaterial, @color baseColor, @color dyeColor) {
  rough = material.roughness(baseMaterial);
  coat = material.withRoughness(material.withMetallic(baseMaterial, 1), sqrt(rough * rough + 0.01));
  return coat, baseColor * dyeColor * 0.9, 0, 0;
}

/// hardAnodized(baseMaterial, baseColor, thickness): hard anodize (Type III),
/// `thickness` um of oxide (25 to 100). Undyed, it greys and darkens toward a
/// bronze-black as it thickens, the metal's glint fading into the film's
/// (judged from 25 um grey to 75 um near black). Returns material, color,
/// height and slope.
hardAnodized(baseMaterial, @color baseColor, @range(25, 100) thickness) {
  depth = clamp((thickness - 25) / 50, 0, 1);
  rough = material.roughness(baseMaterial);
  coat = material.withRoughness(material.withMetallic(baseMaterial, 0.6 - 0.4 * depth), sqrt(rough * rough + 0.09));
  film = color.mixOklab([0.46, 0.45, 0.42], [0.10, 0.10, 0.09], depth);
  return coat, film, 0, 0;
}

/// blackOxide(baseMaterial, oiled): steel blackened by black oxide, a film of
/// magnetite about a micrometre thick. It is too thin to hide the machining,
/// so the lay and grooves are kept, and black, its glint the magnetite's
/// (n about 2.4); the oil it is sealed with (oiled 1) glosses it. Returns
/// material, color, height and slope.
blackOxide(baseMaterial, @range(0, 1) oiled) {
  rough = material.roughness(baseMaterial) * (1 - 0.3 * clamp(oiled, 0, 1));
  coat = material.withIor(material.withRoughness(material.withMetallic(baseMaterial, 0), rough), 2.4);
  return coat, [0.045, 0.045, 0.05], 0, 0;
}

/// phosphate(baseMaterial, manganese): steel phosphated, a crust of crystals
/// a few micrometres across that hides the machining under a matte grey,
/// dark for manganese phosphate (manganese 1) and lighter for zinc (0). The
/// crystals are far too small to see: they scatter the highlight evenly and
/// take the lay with them. Returns material, color, height and slope.
phosphate(baseMaterial, @range(0, 1) manganese) {
  crust = material.withLay(material.withRoughness(material.withMetallic(baseMaterial, 0), 0.8), [0, 0, 0], 0);
  tone = color.mixOklab([0.26, 0.27, 0.28], [0.07, 0.07, 0.075], clamp(manganese, 0, 1));
  return crust, tone, 0, 0;
}

// --- Interference films ---

/// heatTint(baseMaterial, baseColor, celsius): steel tempered at `celsius`,
/// wearing its temper colour: the magnetite film the heat grows (n 2.42),
/// tens of nanometres thick, colours the metal by interference, faint yellow
/// at 176 C, light straw at 205, dark straw at 226, brown at 260, purple at
/// 282, dark blue at 310, light blue at 337 and grey-blue from 371, where the
/// film grows too thick to colour. The film's thickness is read from a table
/// 25 C apart, 29 nm at 175 C to 95 nm at 375 C, set so the model's colours
/// fall at the tempering table's temperatures; they run about 10 nm under the
/// literature's first-order anchors (46 nm light yellow, 72 nm blue), since a
/// real index stands in for the metal's reflection phase. The film follows
/// the surface, so the machining beneath is kept. Returns material, color,
/// height and slope.
heatTint(baseMaterial, @color baseColor, @range(150, 400) celsius) {
  return material.withMetallic(baseMaterial, 1), color.thinFilmOver(temperFilm(celsius), 2.42, baseColor), 0, 0;
}

// The oxide's thickness in nm every 25 C from 175 C, read between rows by
// hat weights, as color.sampleMap reads a palette.
temperFilmTable = [29, 35, 42, 47, 54, 61, 72, 84, 95];

temperFilm(celsius) {
  t = clamp((celsius - 175) / 200, 0, 1);
  weighted = map((d, k) { return d * max(0, 1 - abs(t * 8 - k)); }, temperFilmTable);
  return reduce(0, (acc, d) { return acc + d; }, weighted);
}

/// anodizedTitanium(baseMaterial, baseColor, volts): titanium anodized at
/// `volts`, its oxide (n 2.3, between the 2.1 and 2.5 measured) grown 2 nm
/// per volt (1.6 to 2.8 in the sources) and colouring the metal by
/// interference: bronze and gold by 20 V, purple at 30, blue at 35, pale by
/// 50, the second order's yellow at 60, pink at 80, blue again at 95 and
/// green at 110. The charts disagree on the voltages by half an order, so
/// the rate is the sources' and the colours are the model's. The film
/// follows the surface, so the machining beneath is kept. Returns material,
/// color, height and slope.
anodizedTitanium(baseMaterial, @color baseColor, @range(5, 110) volts) {
  return material.withMetallic(baseMaterial, 1), color.thinFilmOver(2.0 * volts, 2.3, baseColor), 0, 0;
}

// --- Powder coat ---

/// glossRoughness(glossUnits): the roughness of a coat whose 60 degree gloss
/// is `glossUnits` (90 high gloss, 50 satin, 20 low, 5 matte).
///
/// This was its own closed form, the lobe width 0.044 sqrt(100 / GU - 1),
/// judged from a GGX lobe's tail. It read far too rough. Integrating the
/// GGX/Smith/Schlick BRDF over the receptor ISO 2813 specifies -- 4.4 by 11.7
/// degrees at 60 degree incidence, against the black glass standard of index
/// 1.567 -- and asking what each roughness would MEASURE on that meter:
///
///   stated GU   old form reads   material.roughnessFromGloss reads
///          20             16.2                              19.9
///          45             31.4                              49.5
///          70             48.9                              76.4
///          85             64.0                              85.8
///
/// The old form is off by up to 21 gloss units through the range coatings
/// actually live in; the material library's fit, which is numerically
/// integrated rather than judged, is within about 7 at worst and within 1 at
/// both ends. So this now defers to it, and the name is kept because it reads
/// better at the call sites and because a coat's gloss is a coating idea.
glossRoughness(glossUnits) {
  return material.roughnessFromGloss(glossUnits);
}

/// powderCoat(coatColor, glossUnits, peel): a powder coat of `coatColor`
/// whose gloss is `glossUnits`, with orange peel: the coat flows out into
/// gentle waves, `peel` from 0 (flat) to 1 (heavy), which a wave-scan
/// measures in two bands, long waves of a millimetre or two and short ones of
/// a few tenths. A few micrometres high (1.3 um root mean square at peel 1,
/// about 8 um peak to valley), they break a glossy highlight into the peel's
/// dimpled wobble up close; seen smaller than a pixel their slopes widen the
/// lobe instead. The color is the coat's, even: the peel shows in the
/// reflection, not the albedo. Returns material, color, height and slope.
powderCoat(@color coatColor, @range(0, 100) glossUnits, @range(0, 1) peel) {
  amount = clamp(peel, 0, 1);
  // Each band is fine detail (name.detail), worked out only where it shows.
  shortWave = 0;
  shortWave.detail = noise.gradient(0.3) * 0.0006 * amount * texture.fadeDetail(0.3);
  shortWave.detailSize = 0.3;
  waves = 0;
  waves.detail = (noise.gradient(1.5) + 0.5 * noise.gradient(0.65) * texture.fadeDetail(0.65)) * 0.0065 * amount * texture.fadeDetail(1.5) + shortWave;
  waves.detailSize = 1.5;
  rough = glossRoughness(glossUnits);
  alpha = rough * rough;
  // The waves' slopes, lost as they fade: 0.0032 root mean square for the
  // long band and 0.0010 for the short at peel 1, measured by finite
  // differences over four 3 mm patches.
  lobe = texture.reliefRoughness(alpha, 0.0010 * amount, 0.3);
  wide = texture.reliefRoughness(lobe, 0.0032 * amount, 1.5);
  coat = material.withDisplacement(material.make(sqrt(wide), 0, 1.5), waves);
  // Stand-in: the waves too small to see, both bands' slopes in the lobe.
  coat.simple = material.make(sqrt(sqrt(alpha * alpha + 0.00001124 * amount * amount)), 0, 1.5);
  return coat, coatColor, 0, 0;
}

/// wrinkleCoat(coatColor, size): a wrinkle powder coat, its skin cured faster
/// than beneath it and puckered into ridges `size` mm apart (0.3 to 1), low
/// gloss. Returns material, color, height and slope.
wrinkleCoat(@color coatColor, @range(0.3, 1) size) {
  relief = 0;
  relief.detail = (0.234 - noise.turbulence2(size, 0.5, 2.1)) * 0.15 * size * texture.fadeDetail(size);
  relief.detailSize = size;
  alpha = texture.reliefRoughness(0.2, 0.5, size);
  coat = material.withDisplacement(material.make(sqrt(alpha), 0, 1.5), relief);
  // Stand-in: the ridges too small to see, their slopes in the lobe.
  coat.simple = material.make(sqrt(sqrt(0.04 + 0.25)), 0, 1.5);
  return coat, coatColor, 0, 0;
}

/// sandCoat(coatColor, grain): a sand-textured powder coat, grains `grain` mm
/// across (0.1 to 0.3) standing out of a matte coat. Returns material, color,
/// height and slope.
sandCoat(@color coatColor, @range(0.1, 0.3) grain) {
  relief = texture.grainRelief(grain, 0.4 * grain);
  alpha = texture.reliefRoughness(0.3, 0.6, grain);
  coat = material.withDisplacement(material.make(sqrt(alpha), 0, 1.5), relief);
  // Stand-in: the grains too small to see, their slopes in the lobe.
  coat.simple = material.make(sqrt(sqrt(0.09 + 0.36)), 0, 1.5);
  return coat, coatColor, 0, 0;
}

/// hammertone(coatColor, cellSize): a hammertone powder coat, aluminium flake
/// in a coat that sets in dimples `cellSize` mm across (2 to 6), as if beaten:
/// the flake glints and each dimple catches the light its own way. Returns
/// material, color, height and slope.
hammertone(@color coatColor, @range(2, 6) cellSize) {
  dimpleF1, dimpleF2, dimpleCell = noise.cellularCell(cellSize);
  bowl = clamp(dimpleF1 / (0.7 * cellSize), 0, 1);
  relief = 0;
  relief.detail = (bowl * bowl - 0.578) * 0.06 * cellSize * texture.fadeDetail(cellSize);
  relief.detailSize = cellSize;
  shade = 1;
  shade.detail = texture.fadeTo(0.85 + 0.3 * noiseHash3(dimpleCell + [41, 7, 13]).x, 1, cellSize);
  shade.detailSize = cellSize;
  // The dimples' slopes, 0.09 root mean square at any size (measured).
  alpha = texture.reliefRoughness(0.12, 0.09, cellSize);
  coat = material.withDisplacement(material.make(sqrt(alpha), 0.5, 1.5), relief);
  // Stand-in: the dimples too small to see, their slopes in the lobe.
  coat.simple = material.make(sqrt(sqrt(0.0144 + 0.0081)), 0.5, 1.5);
  tint = coatColor * shade;
  tint.simple = coatColor;
  return coat, tint, 0, 0;
}

// --- Paint ---

/// edgeWear(faceA, faceB, width): how worn a part is near the edge where two
/// of its faces meet, from their distances: 1 on the edge, falling to 0
/// `width` mm onto either face. Take the max over the edges that wear, e.g.
/// `wear = max(coating.edgeWear(slabX, slabY, 3), coating.edgeWear(slabY,
/// slabZ, 3));`, and hand it to chippedPaint.
edgeWear(faceA, faceB, width) {
  return 1 - clamp(-min(faceA, faceB) / width, 0, 1);
}

/// chippedPaint(baseMaterial, baseColor, paintColor, primerColor, glossUnits,
/// wear, chipSize): paint of `paintColor` and `glossUnits` gloss over a primer
/// of `primerColor` over the part, chipped as painted steel in service is.
/// The chips are sharp-edged and angular, clustered, and spread over sizes
/// as real chips are, by a power law heavy toward the small: the count of
/// chips wider than d falls as d^-1.7, as measured on two Poly Haven scans
/// of painted plate, so each octave of size holds about the same area of
/// them. `chipSize` (mm) is their typical size, the area-weighted median;
/// the largest run to about three times it and the smallest to a quarter.
/// A shallow chip shows the primer; the deeper ones, mostly the larger,
/// show the bare part within a ring of primer, sunk by the topcoat's 0.05 mm
/// and the primer's 0.03. `wear` (0 to 1, see edgeWear) sets how much is
/// lost: even unworn paint carries a sprinkling, half a percent to one, as
/// the scans do; 0.5 loses about an eighth, and by 1 the paint is gone. The
/// paint itself is faintly mottled and chalked, lighter, grayer and duller
/// in broad patches. Chips too small to see settle to their share. Returns
/// material, color, height and slope.
chippedPaint(baseMaterial, @color baseColor, @color paintColor, @color primerColor, @range(0, 100) glossUnits, wear, @range(0.5, 30) chipSize) {
  reach = chipReach(wear);
  paintGone, primerGone = chipCover(chipSize, reach);
  chalk = paintMottle();
  paintMat = material.make(glossRoughness(glossUnits) + 0.02 * chalk, 0, 1.5);
  primerMat = material.make(0.85, 0, 1.5);
  // The primer shows where the paint is gone and the primer is not: of what
  // the primer still covers, this share. Exact for crisp chips, and it keeps
  // the faded mix the mean of the three.
  primerShows = clamp((paintGone - primerGone) / max(1 - primerGone, 0.0001), 0, 1);
  coated = material.blend(paintMat, primerMat, primerShows);
  worn = material.blend(coated, baseMaterial, primerGone);
  // The coats' steps, less their mean, so the stand-in's flat height is the
  // relief's own average.
  chipped = material.withDisplacement(worn, material.displacement(baseMaterial) + 0.05 * (chipUnion(reach, 0) - paintGone) + 0.03 * (chipUnion(reach, 1) - primerGone));
  chipped.simple = chippedStandInMaterial(baseMaterial, glossUnits, wear);
  // Chalking: lighter, and grayer by the same token, zero on average.
  paintLab = color.rgb2oklab(paintColor);
  dL = 0.009 * chalk;
  mottled = color.oklab2rgb(paintLab + [dL, -8 * dL * paintLab.y, -8 * dL * paintLab.z]);
  coatColor = color.mixOklab(mottled, primerColor, primerShows);
  finish = color.mixOklab(coatColor, baseColor, primerGone);
  finish.simple = chippedStandInColor(baseColor, paintColor, primerColor, wear);
  return chipped, finish, 0, 0;
}

// How far the chips reach into the paint for a `wear`, in standard
// deviations of the chip field: slowly at first and fast toward 1, so an
// edge's wear band is mostly bare at the arris and sparsely chipped across
// most of its width (judged).
chipReach(wear) {
  return 4.2 * wear * wear;
}

// The share of a unit normal variable above y (Bowling et al. 2009, within
// 0.00014).
normalTail(y) {
  return 1 / (1 + exp(1.5976 * y + 0.070566 * y * y * y));
}

// Where octave i (0 the coarsest) chips: its field's threshold, `extra` 1
// for the primer, which only the deeper part of a chip takes. Finer octaves
// chip less often and less deeply, so small chips mostly show primer.
chipEdge(i, reach, extra) {
  return 2.4 + 0.35 * i + (0.15 + 0.25 * i) * extra - reach;
}

// The share of the paint the chips take (extra 0), or of the primer (extra
// 1), all octaves together: their union, which the octaves' clustering
// makes smaller than independent octaves would. Fitted to the mean of
// chipCover over 16000 random points at reach 0 to 4.2, within 0.005.
chipUnion(reach, extra) {
  return extra > 0.5 ? normalTail((2.40 - reach) / 0.98) : normalTail((2.06 - reach) / 0.90);
}

// What one octave leaves: its chips where it shows, and where it is too
// small to see, its part of the union's share, split among the octaves by
// their own shares (perShare is ln(1 - union) over their sum), so the
// octaves faded together settle to the union exactly.
chipKept(z, i, reach, extra, perShare, shown) {
  edge = chipEdge(i, reach, extra);
  target = 1 - exp(perShare * normalTail(edge));
  return 1 - target - (texture.mask(z, edge, 0.04) - target) * shown;
}

// The share lost to the chips of all five octaves at a point.
chipLayer(z0, z1, z2, z3, z4, w0, w1, w2, w3, w4, reach, extra) {
  total = reduce(0, (a, i) { return a + normalTail(chipEdge(i, reach, extra)); }, [0, 1, 2, 3, 4]);
  perShare = ln(max(1 - chipUnion(reach, extra), 0.000001)) / max(total, 0.000001);
  return 1 - chipKept(z0, 0, reach, extra, perShare, w0) * chipKept(z1, 1, reach, extra, perShare, w1) * chipKept(z2, 2, reach, extra, perShare, w2) * chipKept(z3, 3, reach, extra, perShare, w3) * chipKept(z4, 4, reach, extra, perShare, w4);
}

// The paint and the primer the chips take, 0 to 1 each. Each octave of
// size, from four times chipSize down by halves, chips where its own field
// passes a threshold: gradient noise from that octave down, each finer one
// 0.55 as strong, normalized to unit spread over the octaves still shown so
// a threshold cuts the same share at any distance. The coarsest octave is
// shared into the rest (0.35), so small chips cluster about large ones; the
// next octave's |noise| creases each field (0.5), notching the edges; and a
// random step per Voronoi cell chipSize across (0.7) sets the coarse
// octaves' edges along the cell walls, in straight runs and sharp corners,
// as paint breaks. The noise's spread, 0.189, is measured over 4000 points.
chipCover(chipSize, reach) {
  s0 = 4 * chipSize;
  w0 = texture.fadeDetail(s0);
  w1 = texture.fadeDetail(s0 / 2.07);
  w2 = texture.fadeDetail(s0 / 4.21);
  w3 = texture.fadeDetail(s0 / 8.6);
  w4 = texture.fadeDetail(s0 / 17.3);
  f1c, f2c, cell = noise.cellularCell(chipSize);
  facet = 0.7 * (noiseHash3(cell + [29, 7, 43]).x - 0.5) * 3.464;
  n0 = w0 * noise.gradient(s0) / 0.189;
  n1 = w1 * noise.gradient(s0 / 2.07) / 0.189;
  n2 = w2 * noise.gradient(s0 / 4.21) / 0.189;
  n3 = w3 * noise.gradient(s0 / 8.6) / 0.189;
  n4 = w4 * noise.gradient(s0 / 17.3) / 0.189;
  f4 = n4;
  e4 = w4 * w4;
  f3 = n3 + 0.55 * f4;
  e3 = w3 * w3 + 0.3025 * e4;
  f2 = n2 + 0.55 * f3;
  e2 = w2 * w2 + 0.3025 * e3;
  f1 = n1 + 0.55 * f2;
  e1 = w1 * w1 + 0.3025 * e2;
  f0 = n0 + 0.55 * f1;
  e0 = w0 * w0 + 0.3025 * e1;
  // 1 / sqrt(1 + 0.35^2) and 1 / sqrt(1 + 0.7^2), holding each at unit spread;
  // 0.83 is |noise|'s mean, 0.157 / 0.189, so the crease adds nothing on average.
  z0 = (f0 / sqrt(max(e0, 0.000001)) + facet * w2) * 0.8192 - 0.5 * (abs(n1) - 0.83 * w1);
  z1 = ((f1 / sqrt(max(e1, 0.000001)) + 0.35 * n0) * 0.9439 + facet * w2) * 0.8192 - 0.5 * (abs(n2) - 0.83 * w2);
  z2 = ((f2 / sqrt(max(e2, 0.000001)) + 0.35 * n0) * 0.9439 + facet * w2) * 0.8192 - 0.5 * (abs(n3) - 0.83 * w3);
  z3 = (f3 / sqrt(max(e3, 0.000001)) + 0.35 * n0) * 0.9439 - 0.5 * (abs(n4) - 0.83 * w4);
  z4 = (f4 / sqrt(max(e4, 0.000001)) + 0.35 * n0) * 0.9439;
  return chipLayer(z0, z1, z2, z3, z4, w0, w1, w2, w3, w4, reach, 0), chipLayer(z0, z1, z2, z3, z4, w0, w1, w2, w3, w4, reach, 1);
}

// The paint's mottle, unit spread and zero mean: broad patches 80 mm across
// and finer ones 12, as near equal in strength as the scans' spectra are
// flat (0.006 of lightness per octave from 1 to 300 mm on a weathered blue
// plate; 0.003 on a fresh green container).
paintMottle() {
  return (noise.gradient(80) + 0.8 * noise.gradient(12) * texture.fadeDetail(12)) / (0.189 * 1.28);
}

// chippedPaint's stand-ins: the paint, primer and part mixed by the shares
// the chips take of each.
chippedStandInMaterial(baseMaterial, glossUnits, wear) {
  reach = chipReach(wear);
  share1 = chipUnion(reach, 0);
  share2 = chipUnion(reach, 1);
  coats = material.blend(material.make(glossRoughness(glossUnits), 0, 1.5), material.make(0.85, 0, 1.5), (share1 - share2) / max(1 - share2, 0.0001));
  return material.withDisplacement(material.blend(coats, baseMaterial, share2), material.displacement(baseMaterial));
}

chippedStandInColor(baseColor, paintColor, primerColor, wear) {
  reach = chipReach(wear);
  share1 = chipUnion(reach, 0);
  share2 = chipUnion(reach, 1);
  return color.oklab2rgb(color.rgb2oklab(paintColor) * (1 - share1) + color.rgb2oklab(primerColor) * (share1 - share2) + color.rgb2oklab(baseColor) * share2);
}

// --- Galvanizing ---

/// galvanized(spangleSize, age): hot-dip galvanized steel, its zinc frozen
/// into spangle, crystals `spangleSize` mm across (1 to 10 regular, under 1
/// minimized). Each crystal's grains lie its own way, so it takes a lay of
/// its own and catches the light alone, bright or dark as the part turns,
/// between thin grain boundaries. `age` from 0 (fresh) to 1 dulls it to the
/// even grey of weathered zinc. Crystals too small to see settle to their
/// average, the lay's anisotropy kept with no direction to streak. Returns
/// material, color, height and slope.
galvanized(@range(1, 10) spangleSize, @range(0, 1) age) {
  spangleF1, spangleF2, spangleCell = noise.cellularCell(spangleSize);
  grain = noiseHash3(spangleCell + [17, 53, 5]);
  across = normalize(grain - [0.5, 0.5, 0.5] + [0.0001, 0, 0]);
  fade = texture.fadeDetail(spangleSize);
  stretch = texture.fadeTo(0.3 + 0.5 * grain.y, 0.55, spangleSize);
  rough = texture.fadeTo(0.25 + 0.15 * grain.z, 0.325, spangleSize);
  boundary = texture.coverageLine(spangleF2 - spangleF1, 0.02 * spangleSize);
  crystal = material.withLay(material.make(rough, 1, 1.5), across * fade, stretch);
  weathered = material.make(0.65, 1, 1.5);
  zinc = material.blend(crystal, weathered, clamp(age, 0, 1));
  zinc.simple = galvanizedFlat(age);
  bright = texture.fadeTo(0.92 + 0.16 * grain.x, 1, spangleSize) * (1 - 0.5 * boundary);
  fresh = [0.80, 0.82, 0.84] * bright;
  tone = color.mixOklab(fresh, [0.58, 0.60, 0.61], clamp(age, 0, 1));
  tone.simple = color.mixOklab([0.80, 0.82, 0.84], [0.58, 0.60, 0.61], clamp(age, 0, 1));
  return zinc, tone, 0, 0;
}

// galvanizedFlat(age): galvanizing's stand-in, the spangle settled to its
// average at `age`.
galvanizedFlat(age) {
  return material.blend(material.withLay(material.make(0.325, 1, 1.5), [0, 0, 0], 0.55), material.make(0.65, 1, 1.5), clamp(age, 0, 1));
}

/// galvanized(baseMaterial, spangleSize, age, build, underSize): hot-dip
/// galvanizing over a part: the zinc's spangle, `build` mm of it over the
/// part's relief `underSize` mm across, which it levels as a flowed film does
/// (levelled). The zinc is opaque, so its color replaces the part's. Returns
/// material, color, height and slope.
galvanized(baseMaterial, @range(1, 10) spangleSize, @range(0, 1) age, build, underSize) {
  zinc, tone, height, slope = galvanized(spangleSize, age);
  result = levelled(zinc, baseMaterial, build, underSize);
  result.simple = levelled(galvanizedFlat(age), baseMaterial, build, underSize);
  return result, tone, 0, 0;
}

// --- Flowed films over a prepared surface ---
//
// A conversion coating grows out of the metal and follows it, so the machining
// beneath shows through however thick it is: that is why anodized and
// blackOxide keep the lay and take no account of what is under them. A film
// that FLOWS does the opposite. It pools in the valleys and levels what is
// finer than it is thick, so the same film fills a brushed lay and leaves an
// as-cast skin standing proud. Levelling therefore needs two numbers the coat
// alone cannot supply: how thick the film is, and how coarse the surface under
// it is.
//
// `underSize` is that second number, the wavelength in mm of the relief the
// process beneath left — a turned surface's feed, a brushed lay's grain size,
// an as-cast skin's nodules. It is passed in rather than read off the base
// material because a material record carries roughness, lay and displacement
// but not the scale they came from.
//
// The share surviving is material.filmKeeps, whose coefficient was fitted to
// wood finishes over sanded, sawn and planed surfaces; it is judged to carry
// over, the mechanism being the same film flowing into the same valleys.

/// levelled(coat, baseMaterial, build, underSize): `coat` laid as a flowed
/// film `build` mm thick over `baseMaterial`, whose relief is `underSize` mm
/// across. What is left of the surface beneath survives in three ways at once,
/// all governed by the one share: its displacement is cut by it, its lay's
/// anisotropy by it, and its lobe's variance by its square, variance going as
/// amplitude squared. The coat's own metallic and index are kept, so a clear
/// film passes them through and an opaque one replaces them.
levelled(coat, baseMaterial, build, underSize) {
  keeps = material.filmKeeps(underSize, build);
  coatRough = material.roughness(coat);
  baseRough = material.roughness(baseMaterial);
  coatAlpha = coatRough * coatRough;
  baseAlpha = baseRough * baseRough;
  alpha = sqrt(coatAlpha * coatAlpha + keeps * keeps * baseAlpha * baseAlpha);
  relief = material.displacement(coat) + material.displacement(baseMaterial) * keeps;
  lobe = material.withRoughness(coat, sqrt(alpha));
  return material.withDisplacement(material.withLay(lobe, material.lay(baseMaterial), material.anisotropy(baseMaterial) * keeps), relief);
}

/// clearCoat(baseMaterial, baseColor, tint, glossUnits, build, underSize): a
/// clear film over the part — a urethane, lacquer or clear powder — of `tint`
/// (white for none) and 60 degree gloss `glossUnits`, `build` mm thick (0.03 a
/// shellac, 0.08 three coats of polyurethane, 0.12 a clear powder), flowed
/// over a surface whose relief is `underSize` mm across. The film fills what
/// is finer than it is thick: a 0.08 mm urethane leaves 1.2% of a P120 brushed
/// lay (0.133 mm) and 1.6% of a lathe's 0.15 mm feed, so the turning all but
/// disappears, but 16% of a 0.5 mm as-cast skin; a 0.002 mm wax over that same
/// lathe finish leaves it untouched.
///
/// The light passes through the film to the metal, so the part keeps its own
/// colour, metallic and index, dimmed a little by the film. What one lobe
/// cannot carry is the film's own reflection, which is a dielectric's and so
/// white where the metal's is coloured: a coated metal's highlight is tinted
/// here where it should have a white film glint over it. That wants a second
/// lobe, which the material record has no row for yet.
/// Returns material, color, height and slope.
clearCoat(baseMaterial, @color baseColor, @color tint, @range(0, 100) glossUnits, build, underSize) {
  bare = material.withLay(material.withRoughness(baseMaterial, glossRoughness(glossUnits)), [0, 0, 0], 0);
  film = material.withDisplacement(bare, 0);
  return levelled(film, baseMaterial, build, underSize), baseColor * tint * 0.97, 0, 0;
}

// The powder coats above build their coat from nothing, so the surface under
// them cannot show through: a coat over a mirror-polished part draws the same
// as one over an as-cast skin. Each has an overload below that takes the part
// it is sprayed onto and levels it, as clearCoat does. A powder coat is opaque,
// so the colour stays the coat's; what survives is the surface — the lay the
// machining left, and its relief.
//
// `build` is the film's thickness in mm: 0.06 a thin powder coat, 0.08 typical,
// 0.12 a heavy one. `underSize` is the wavelength of the relief beneath, a
// turned surface's feed or an as-cast skin's nodules.
//
// Each declares its own stand-in, levelled too. The surviving lay is cheap —
// a direction and a number, no texture — so it is worth keeping below the
// Textures rung even where the coat's own waves are dropped.

/// powderCoat(baseMaterial, coatColor, glossUnits, peel, build, underSize):
/// a powder coat sprayed over a prepared surface, which shows through where
/// the coat is too thin to fill it. Otherwise powderCoat(coatColor,
/// glossUnits, peel). Returns material, color, height and slope.
powderCoat(baseMaterial, @color coatColor, @range(0, 100) glossUnits, @range(0, 1) peel, build, underSize) {
  coat, tone, height, slope = powderCoat(coatColor, glossUnits, peel);
  amount = clamp(peel, 0, 1);
  rough = glossRoughness(glossUnits);
  flat = material.make(sqrt(sqrt(rough * rough * rough * rough + 0.00001124 * amount * amount)), 0, 1.5);
  result = levelled(coat, baseMaterial, build, underSize);
  result.simple = levelled(flat, baseMaterial, build, underSize);
  return result, tone, height, slope;
}

/// wrinkleCoat(baseMaterial, coatColor, size, build, underSize): a wrinkle
/// powder coat over a prepared surface. Its ridges are coarse, so a surface
/// under it has to be coarser still to read through them. Otherwise
/// wrinkleCoat(coatColor, size). Returns material, color, height and slope.
wrinkleCoat(baseMaterial, @color coatColor, @range(0.3, 1) size, build, underSize) {
  coat, tone, height, slope = wrinkleCoat(coatColor, size);
  result = levelled(coat, baseMaterial, build, underSize);
  result.simple = levelled(material.make(sqrt(sqrt(0.04 + 0.25)), 0, 1.5), baseMaterial, build, underSize);
  return result, tone, height, slope;
}

/// sandCoat(baseMaterial, coatColor, grain, build, underSize): a sand-textured
/// powder coat over a prepared surface. Otherwise sandCoat(coatColor, grain).
/// Returns material, color, height and slope.
sandCoat(baseMaterial, @color coatColor, @range(0.1, 0.3) grain, build, underSize) {
  coat, tone, height, slope = sandCoat(coatColor, grain);
  result = levelled(coat, baseMaterial, build, underSize);
  result.simple = levelled(material.make(sqrt(sqrt(0.09 + 0.36)), 0, 1.5), baseMaterial, build, underSize);
  return result, tone, height, slope;
}

/// hammertone(baseMaterial, coatColor, cellSize, build, underSize): a
/// hammertone powder coat over a prepared surface. Otherwise
/// hammertone(coatColor, cellSize). Returns material, color, height and slope.
hammertone(baseMaterial, @color coatColor, @range(2, 6) cellSize, build, underSize) {
  coat, tone, height, slope = hammertone(coatColor, cellSize);
  result = levelled(coat, baseMaterial, build, underSize);
  result.simple = levelled(material.make(sqrt(sqrt(0.0144 + 0.0081)), 0.5, 1.5), baseMaterial, build, underSize);
  return result, tone, height, slope;
}

// ===========================================================================
// Ready-made
// ===========================================================================

// One surface from each constructor above, with the arguments the examples
// use, so the material browser can show it. The constructors stay the way to
// set the parameters; see doc/material_composition.md for the plan to adjust
// them from the browser.

/// Red powder coat at 85 gloss units, a little orange peel. Returns material
/// and colour.
@material redPowderCoat() {
  coat, tone, height, slope = powderCoat([0.62, 0.06, 0.05], 85, 0.7);
  return coat, tone;
}

/// Black wrinkle finish, 0.6 mm wrinkles. Returns material and colour.
@material blackWrinkleCoat() {
  coat, tone, height, slope = wrinkleCoat([0.04, 0.04, 0.045], 0.6);
  return coat, tone;
}

/// Green sand-texture coat, 0.2 mm grain. Returns material and colour.
@material greenSandCoat() {
  coat, tone, height, slope = sandCoat([0.12, 0.22, 0.12], 0.2);
  return coat, tone;
}

/// Blue hammertone, 4 mm hammer marks. Returns material and colour.
@material blueHammertone() {
  coat, tone, height, slope = hammertone([0.18, 0.32, 0.45], 4);
  return coat, tone;
}

/// Hot-dip galvanized steel, fresh: bright 1 mm spangles. Returns material and
/// colour.
@material newGalvanized() {
  zinc, tone, height, slope = galvanized(1, 0);
  return zinc, tone;
}

/// Hot-dip galvanized steel, aged to a dull grey. Returns material and colour.
@material agedGalvanized() {
  zinc, tone, height, slope = galvanized(1, 0.7);
  return zinc, tone;
}

/// Steel tempered to light straw, 205 C. Returns material and colour.
@material strawTemperedSteel() {
  steelMat, steelColor = metal.steel();
  coat, tone, height, slope = heatTint(steelMat, steelColor, 205);
  return coat, tone;
}

/// Steel tempered to dark blue, 310 C. Returns material and colour.
@material blueTemperedSteel() {
  steelMat, steelColor = metal.steel();
  coat, tone, height, slope = heatTint(steelMat, steelColor, 310);
  return coat, tone;
}

/// Titanium anodized gold, 20 V. Returns material and colour.
@material goldAnodizedTitanium() {
  titaniumMat, titaniumColor = metal.titanium();
  coat, tone, height, slope = anodizedTitanium(titaniumMat, titaniumColor, 20);
  return coat, tone;
}

/// Titanium anodized blue, 35 V. Returns material and colour.
@material blueAnodizedTitanium() {
  titaniumMat, titaniumColor = metal.titanium();
  coat, tone, height, slope = anodizedTitanium(titaniumMat, titaniumColor, 35);
  return coat, tone;
}

/// Titanium anodized green, 110 V. Returns material and colour.
@material greenAnodizedTitanium() {
  titaniumMat, titaniumColor = metal.titanium();
  coat, tone, height, slope = anodizedTitanium(titaniumMat, titaniumColor, 110);
  return coat, tone;
}

// One @surface overload per operator above, for the Bodies panel to wrap a
// surface with: the slots a document supplies (the part's surface and its
// colour) and the arguments the examples use for the rest. Adjust opens one
// back into the full form; see doc/material_composition.md, "Design:
// operators as presets".

/// anodized(baseMaterial, baseColor): clear anodize. Returns material, color,
/// height and slope.
@surface anodized(baseMaterial, @color baseColor) {
  coat, tone, height, slope = anodized(baseMaterial, baseColor, [1, 1, 1]);
  return coat, tone, height, slope;
}

/// hardAnodized(baseMaterial, baseColor): 50 um hard anodize. Returns
/// material, color, height and slope.
@surface hardAnodized(baseMaterial, @color baseColor) {
  coat, tone, height, slope = hardAnodized(baseMaterial, baseColor, 50);
  return coat, tone, height, slope;
}

/// blackOxide(baseMaterial): oiled black oxide. Returns material, color,
/// height and slope.
@surface blackOxide(baseMaterial) {
  coat, tone, height, slope = blackOxide(baseMaterial, 1);
  return coat, tone, height, slope;
}

/// phosphate(baseMaterial): manganese phosphate. Returns material, color,
/// height and slope.
@surface phosphate(baseMaterial) {
  coat, tone, height, slope = phosphate(baseMaterial, 1);
  return coat, tone, height, slope;
}

/// heatTint(baseMaterial, baseColor): tempered to blue, 310 C. Returns
/// material, color, height and slope.
@surface heatTint(baseMaterial, @color baseColor) {
  coat, tone, height, slope = heatTint(baseMaterial, baseColor, 310);
  return coat, tone, height, slope;
}

/// anodizedTitanium(baseMaterial, baseColor): anodized at 35 V, blue.
/// Returns material, color, height and slope.
@surface anodizedTitanium(baseMaterial, @color baseColor) {
  coat, tone, height, slope = anodizedTitanium(baseMaterial, baseColor, 35);
  return coat, tone, height, slope;
}

/// chippedPaint(baseMaterial, baseColor): green paint at 60 gloss units over
/// a red oxide primer, chipped evenly in 1.5 mm chips. The full form takes
/// the wear as a field (edgeWear). Returns material, color, height and slope.
@surface chippedPaint(baseMaterial, @color baseColor) {
  coat, tone, height, slope = chippedPaint(baseMaterial, baseColor, [0.14, 0.32, 0.18], [0.55, 0.22, 0.14], 60, 0.5, 1.5);
  return coat, tone, height, slope;
}

/// clearCoat(baseMaterial, baseColor): an untinted clear coat at 45 gloss
/// units, 0.08 mm of film over relief 1.2 mm across. Returns material, color,
/// height and slope.
@surface clearCoat(baseMaterial, @color baseColor) {
  coat, tone, height, slope = clearCoat(baseMaterial, baseColor, [1, 1, 1], 45, 0.08, 1.2);
  return coat, tone, height, slope;
}

/// galvanized(baseMaterial): fresh hot-dip galvanizing, 1 mm spangles, 0.085 mm
/// of zinc (ASTM A123's coating grade 85, judged typical) over relief 1.2 mm
/// across; the zinc's color replaces the part's. Returns material, color,
/// height and slope.
@surface galvanized(baseMaterial) {
  coat, tone, height, slope = galvanized(baseMaterial, 1, 0, 0.085, 1.2);
  return coat, tone, height, slope;
}

/// powderCoat(baseMaterial): red powder coat at 85 gloss units with a little
/// orange peel, 0.08 mm of film over relief 1.2 mm across; the coat's colour
/// replaces the part's. Returns material, color, height and slope.
@surface powderCoat(baseMaterial) {
  coat, tone, height, slope = powderCoat(baseMaterial, [0.62, 0.06, 0.05], 85, 0.7, 0.08, 1.2);
  return coat, tone, height, slope;
}

/// wrinkleCoat(baseMaterial): black wrinkle coat, ridges 0.6 mm apart, 0.08
/// mm of film over relief 1.2 mm across; the coat's colour replaces the
/// part's. Returns material, color, height and slope.
@surface wrinkleCoat(baseMaterial) {
  coat, tone, height, slope = wrinkleCoat(baseMaterial, [0.04, 0.04, 0.045], 0.6, 0.08, 1.2);
  return coat, tone, height, slope;
}

/// sandCoat(baseMaterial): green sand-textured coat, 0.2 mm grains, 0.08 mm
/// of film over relief 1.2 mm across; the coat's colour replaces the part's.
/// Returns material, color, height and slope.
@surface sandCoat(baseMaterial) {
  coat, tone, height, slope = sandCoat(baseMaterial, [0.12, 0.22, 0.12], 0.2, 0.08, 1.2);
  return coat, tone, height, slope;
}

/// hammertone(baseMaterial): blue hammertone, 4 mm dimples, 0.08 mm of film
/// over relief 1.2 mm across; the coat's colour replaces the part's. Returns
/// material, color, height and slope.
@surface hammertone(baseMaterial) {
  coat, tone, height, slope = hammertone(baseMaterial, [0.18, 0.32, 0.45], 4, 0.08, 1.2);
  return coat, tone, height, slope;
}

// References
//   BYK-Gardner. Wave-scan: orange peel and DOI measurement.
//   ISO 2813:2014. Paints and varnishes: specular gloss at 20, 60, 85 degrees.
//   MIL-A-8625F. Anodic coatings for aluminum and aluminum alloys.
//   Sere, P. R. et al. (1999). Relationship between texture and corrosion
//     resistance in hot-dip galvanized steel sheets. Surface and Coatings
//     Technology.
//   Tempering (metallurgy), "Tempering colors". Wikipedia. The temper colours
//     and their temperatures. https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tempering_(metallurgy)
//   Peet, M., Bhadeshia, H. K. D. H. Oxide on steel and tempering colours.
//     University of Cambridge. The colours as interference, after Evans
//     (1925). https://www.phase-trans.msm.cam.ac.uk/2008/Oxide/Oxide.html
//   finishing.com, letter 453/44. Blue as first-order interference from
//     about 0.07 um of oxide on steel. https://finishing.com/453/44.shtml
//   Magnetite's index, 2.42, from the materials table at
//     https://steeldata.info/etch/demo/help/compounds/87.html
//   Ohtsuka, T., Masuda, M., Sato, N. (1985). Ellipsometric study of anodic
//     oxide films on titanium in hydrochloric acid, sulfuric acid, and
//     phosphate solution. Journal of the Electrochemical Society, 132(4),
//     787-792. The oxide's index about 2.1 at 546 nm and 2.4 to 2.8 nm/V.
//     https://doi.org/10.1149/1.2097244
//   Titanium anodizing: how it works. Urban EDC. 1.6 to 2.0 nm per volt and
//     the colours by thickness and voltage.
//     https://urbanedc.com/blogs/analog-field-guide/titanium-anodizing-how-it-works
//   Titanium anodizing. Xometry. The colours by voltage.
//     https://www.xometry.com/resources/machining/titanium-anodizing
